### Please describe the problem in your own words.

Currently the command `git annex info` fails   when run inside a submodule that is checked out in a linked Git worktree. The error it provides is:

`changeWorkingDirectory: does not exist `

Ordinary Git commands recognize the worktree correctly, and the same annex command succeeds when `GIT_DIR` and `GIT_WORK_TREE` are explicitly set to absolute paths. 

I think it should work without those overrides.

### What steps will reproduce the problem?


This bash script should reproduce the problem on most systems:

```
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

workdir=$(mktemp -d)
cd "$workdir"
workdir=$(pwd -P)

# Create a repository to use as a submodule.
git init source
git -C source commit --allow-empty -m "Initial commit"

# Add it to a parent repository.
git init main
git -C main -c protocol.file.allow=always submodule add "$workdir/source" sub
git -C main commit -m "Add submodule"

# Check out the submodule in a linked worktree.
git -C main worktree add -b linked ../linked
git -C linked -c protocol.file.allow=always submodule update --init
cd linked/sub
git annex init repro

# Ordinary Git recognizes the worktree.
git rev-parse --show-toplevel
git status

# This fails with "changeWorkingDirectory: does not exist".
# Continue so we can also demonstrate the workaround.
git annex info || true

# Explicit absolute paths make the same command succeed.
GIT_DIR=$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir) \
GIT_WORK_TREE=$(pwd -P) \
git annex info
```

### What version of git-annex are you using? On what operating system?

I have tried it across a range of versions.

